Expected Outcomes
A working eBPF-based tracing pipeline using syscount and gethostlatency that captures live kernel-level activity into text logs
Two purpose-built Python analyzers (analyze_syscalls.py, analyze_dns_latency.py) that turn raw eBPF trace output into readable statistics and performance warnings
A consolidated performance_report.json produced by generate_performance_report.py, combining syscall and DNS metrics into a single machine-readable artifact with concrete recommendations
Conclusion
In this lab, students used eBPF-based tracing tools, syscount and gethostlatency, to observe live kernel-level activity, including system call frequency and DNS resolution latency, without modifying or recompiling any application code. Working through the tasks demonstrated how raw eBPF trace output can be captured into log files, parsed with Python, and turned into structured statistics that highlight concrete issues such as excessive I/O system calls or slow DNS lookups. The final performance report generator showed how these two independent data sources can be merged into a single JSON artifact with severity-ranked issues and prioritized recommendations, a pattern directly applicable to production incident response and capacity planning. Students who want to go further should explore additional BCC tools such as opensnoop, execsnoop, and biolatency, integrate this reporting pipeline into a scheduled monitoring job, and experiment with writing custom eBPF programs using the bcc Python bindings to trace application-specific code paths.
